1. Cartesian product

This is when every row from the first table is combined with every row from the second table.

Example:

	SELECT *
	FROM A, B;

If A has 2 rows and B has 3 rows, the result will have 6 rows.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================


2. Equal join

This is a join where rows are matched using the = condition.

Example:

	SELECT *
	FROM A
	JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;

It shows only the rows where the key values match.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================


3. Outer join

This shows not only matching rows, but also rows that exist in only one of the tables.

There are three main types:

3.1 LEFT OUTER JOIN

Example: 

	SELECT *
	FROM A
	LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;

Shows all rows from A and matching rows from B.

3.2 RIGHT OUTER JOIN

Example:

	SELECT *
	FROM A
	RIGHT JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;

Shows all rows from B and matching rows from A.

3.3 FULL OUTER JOIN

Example:

	SELECT *
	FROM A
	FULL OUTER JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;

Shows all rows from both tables.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================

4. Self join

A table is joined to itself.

Example:

	SELECT *
	FROM Employees e1
	JOIN Employees e2 ON e1.manager_id = e2.id;

This is useful, for example, for finding an employee and their manager.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================

5. Join based on inequality

The join is done not with =, but with >, <, <>, and so on.

Example:

	SELECT *
	FROM A
	JOIN B ON A.value > B.value;

It is used less often, for example for ranges or comparisons.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================

6. Union

Combines the results of two queries into one list.

Example:

	SELECT name FROM A
	UNION
	SELECT name FROM B;

UNION usually removes duplicates.

If you want to keep duplicates:

Example: 

	SELECT name FROM A
	UNION ALL
	SELECT name FROM B;

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================

7. Intersect

Shows only the rows that exist in both the first and the second query.

Example:

	SELECT name FROM A
	INTERSECT
	SELECT name FROM B;

That is, only the common values.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================

8. Minus

Shows rows that exist in the first query but not in the second.

Example:

	SELECT name FROM A
	MINUS
	SELECT name FROM B;

In some databases, EXCEPT is used instead of MINUS.

## ==================================================================================
## ==================================================================================

9. Duplicates

These are repeated values in the result.

Example:

	SELECT name
	FROM A;

If table A contains repeated names, they will also appear in the result.

To remove duplicates:

Example:

	SELECT DISTINCT name
	FROM A;